# RaceGOW PVC Track Set — build123d edition

Python/build123d port of the earlier OpenSCAD RaceGOW connector project. It keeps the same CTC convention, connector topology and manufacturing logic, but moves the CAD model to a real BREP/OpenCascade workflow and exports both **STEP** and **STL**.

The project targets **build123d 0.13.0**. build123d's official documentation describes it as a Pythonic BREP framework over Open Cascade, with algebraic object operations and STL/STEP export.

## Core convention: Gate size is Center-to-Center

`gate_ctc` always means the distance between connector centers.

```text
PVC cut length = gate_ctc - 2 × pipe_stop_offset
```

With the default 25 mm PVC and `pipe_stop_offset = pipe_od / 2`, the PVC piece is exactly **25 mm shorter than the CTC dimension**.

## Default stock-optimized result

The defaults are intentionally optimized for your 2000 mm stock pipes and a 2 mm cutter kerf:

```text
Gate CTC:                  690.33 mm
PVC cut length:            665.33 mm
Pieces per 2000 mm pipe:        3
Stock pipes required:           7
Segments produced:             21
Segments required:             20
Spare segments:                 1
Offcut/full used stock:      0.00 mm (theoretical)
```

Always measure the real cutting kerf before committing all stock.

## RaceGOW connector BOM

- 4 × `elbow`
- 6 × `tee`
- 6 × `4way`
- 2 × `5way`

Optional: `cross` (four horizontal ports).

Port topology:

```text
elbow : +X +Y
tee   : -X +X +Y
cross : -X +X -Y +Y
4way  : -X +X +Y +Z
5way  : -X +X -Y +Y +Z
```

## Why this structure is useful with Codex

```text
src/racegow/config.py         dimensions + derived values
src/racegow/connectors.py     generic connector geometry
src/racegow/track.py          complete-set preview assembly
src/racegow/manufacturing.py  BOM + PVC cut plan
src/racegow/exporting.py      STL/STEP validation + exports
parts/                        tiny viewer wrappers
scripts/build.py              one production command
tests/                        numeric + geometry regression tests
build/                        generated manufacturing/CAD outputs
AGENTS.md                     rules for AI coding agents
```

The manufacturing calculations do not depend on build123d, so cut lists/BOMs can be generated even before CAD dependencies are installed.

## Setup (VS Code + OCP CAD Viewer + Codex)

The repository is configured for a project-local `.venv`. After cloning/opening
the folder in VS Code, install the recommended extensions when VS Code offers
them (`Python`, `Pylance`, `OCP CAD Viewer`, `Codex`).

### One-command environment setup

On Manjaro/Linux, run the VS Code task:

```text
Ctrl+Shift+P → Tasks: Run Task → RaceGOW: Setup .venv
```

Or from a terminal in the repository:

```bash
python scripts/setup_env.py
```

This creates `.venv/` and installs the project plus development/viewer extras.
VS Code is configured to use:

```text
${workspaceFolder}/.venv/bin/python
```

If VS Code had an old interpreter cached, select it once with:

```text
Ctrl+Shift+P → Python: Select Interpreter → .venv/bin/python
```

The `viewer` extra installs `ocp_vscode` plus `watchfiles`; the latter powers
the automatic live rebuild workflow. The OCP CAD Viewer extension and the
`ocp_vscode` Python package should use the same major/minor release. This
project currently targets OCP CAD Viewer / `ocp_vscode` 4.1.x.

### Manual setup equivalent

```bash
python -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,viewer]"
```

## Live viewing in OCP CAD Viewer

The preferred development loop is a persistent watcher. Start the OCP CAD
Viewer, then run:

```text
Ctrl+Shift+P → Tasks: Run Task → RaceGOW: Live view (choose part)
```

Choose `5way`, `4way`, `tee`, `elbow`, `cross`, or `fit_tests`. The task stays
running. Whenever a Python file under `src/` or `scripts/` is saved, the model
is rebuilt in a fresh Python child process and sent to the already-open viewer.
The camera is kept between updates so you can rotate to an interesting detail
and continue editing. Stop the watcher with `Ctrl+C` in its terminal.

Terminal equivalent:

```bash
.venv/bin/python scripts/live_view.py 5way
```

For a single refresh instead of live mode:

```text
Ctrl+Shift+P → Tasks: Run Task → RaceGOW: View once (choose part)
```

or:

```bash
.venv/bin/python scripts/view.py 5way
```

The repository also includes `.vscode/launch.json`, so `F5` / Run and Debug can
launch either a one-shot viewer or the live watcher with a selectable part.

## First: fit calibration

Build everything:

```bash
python scripts/build.py --preview
```

Then print `build/fit_tests.stl`. The coupons cover these diametral clearances:

```text
0.20 / 0.30 / 0.40 / 0.50 / 0.60 mm
```

Choose the fit you want and update this value in `src/racegow/config.py`:

```python
diametral_clearance: float = 0.40
```

## Production build

Geometry validation only:

```bash
python scripts/build.py --check
```

Tests:

```bash
python -m pytest
```

Export required RaceGOW parts as STL + STEP:

```bash
python scripts/build.py
```

Also export the optional cross and a STEP preview of the complete connector set:

```bash
python scripts/build.py --with-cross --preview
```

Generate only BOM/cut-list/manufacturing files, without importing build123d:

```bash
python scripts/build.py --manufacturing-only
```

## Generated manufacturing files

`build/` contains:

- `MANUFACTURING.md` — human-readable plan
- `BOM.csv` — Bambu Studio object quantities
- `CUT_LIST.csv` — one row per stock pipe
- `plan.json` — machine-readable derived dimensions
- `config_snapshot.json` — exact input configuration used
- `*.stl` — slicer files after a CAD build
- `*.step` — editable CAD/BREP files after a CAD build

For Bambu Studio, import each connector STL **once** and set the quantity according to `BOM.csv`. This keeps the CAD repository independent of a particular build plate layout.

## Changing gate size

To use a fixed CTC instead of automatic stock optimization:

```python
optimize_gate_for_stock: bool = False
gate_ctc_manual: float = 660.0
```

At 660 mm CTC with the default 25 mm stop geometry, the PVC pieces become 635 mm long.

## VS Code tasks included

- `RaceGOW: Setup .venv` — create/update the local Python environment
- `RaceGOW: Live view (choose part)` — rebuild the selected model on save
- `RaceGOW: View once (choose part)` — send one model to OCP CAD Viewer
- `RaceGOW: Run tests` — run pytest
- `RaceGOW: Check build123d geometry` — validate CAD geometry
- `RaceGOW: Build STL + STEP` — default `Ctrl+Shift+B` production build

The `parts/*.py` files remain deliberately tiny wrappers. Shared geometry stays
in `src/racegow/`, so Codex changes remain easy to review and test.
